Z||000005||GT GT 87||05-22-2001||09:52 PM||||I just bought the balanced rotating parts from shaun 41178 and he paid 200$ for the balance job.  When your machinist puts it all together , that is the rest of the blueprint.  Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||05-23-2001||01:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Scott:<BR><B>Basically everthing is checked wtih various calipers and assembled for optimum clearances.  For example, if one cylinder is bored just slightly larger than the rest (within a few thousanths of an inch), the piston with the largest diameter is installed into this cylinder  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, the piston is measured and marked for a specific bore, then the bore is honed to the size of the piston.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||05-23-2001||10:14 PM||||Every engine builder follows a different drummer...<BR>Some actually try to go by factory specs, others use their own specs...<BR>My experience is to try and get as tight a clearance on bearings and pistons as posible while still allowing for good oil flow...<BR>If you bore out the cylinders 60 tousanths, it can not be "factory spec" any more, so trash the engine?<BR>That is where I started with my SBC 350...<BR>A good engine build up (NOT REBUILD) starts with good machine work to the block and heads, then the internals are worked on to mate them to the block and heads...<BR>That is blue printing...<BR>Now balancing is mating the rotating assemblies (crank, rods, and pistons)<BR>so there is no/low vibration...<BR>It is important at high rpms more than low, but the wrong vibration at low rpm can tear up an engine too...<BR> That is why there is a harmonic balancer...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
